# In-Pod Kubernetes API Server Interrogation & Resource Navigation

Comprehensive hands-on guide for interrogating the Kubernetes API server directly from within a running pod or via
`kubectl proxy`, navigating core and named API paths, inspecting resource payloads, and decoding secrets.

---

## 1. Overview & Mechanics

Applications and controller agents running inside Kubernetes often need to inspect cluster state or read configuration
and secrets without relying on an external `kubectl` binary. Kubernetes automatically provisions identities and
certificates to pods for this purpose.

### In-Pod Credential Projection

Every container that runs in a pod with `automountServiceAccountToken: true` (the default) receives a projected volume
mounted at `/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/`:

| File Path | Description | Usage in API Requests |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `token` | OIDC signed JWT bearer token representing the pod's ServiceAccount identity | `Authorization: Bearer <token>` |
| `ca.crt` | Public CA certificate of the cluster's kube-apiserver | `curl --cacert /path/to/ca.crt ...` |
| `namespace` | Text file containing the namespace where the pod was scheduled | Injected into scoped path URL |

### API Server Address Resolution

Inside the pod's network namespace, kube-apiserver can be addressed via:

- **DNS**: `https://kubernetes.default.svc` or `https://kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local`
- **Environment Variables**: `https://${KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST}:${KUBERNETES_SERVICE_PORT}` (defaults to IP `10.96.0.1` and port `443`)

